      "writtenOn": "2025-01-06T17:31:28Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Is it guaranteed that all the members we skip are at the end of the structure? Otherwise it\u0027ll mess up the indices used in `access` instructions. We should `TINT_ASSERT` to make sure, or fixup the indices if that\u0027s not the case.",
      "revId": "f1c28fe8671ffba7ea7b5411b6ed0615a140d9b2",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7ef9dce7_13293831",
        "filename": "src/tint/lang/spirv/reader/lower/shader_io.cc",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 201,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000257
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-01-08T21:05:49Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I renamed this to `ShouldSkipMemberEmission`. We still create the struct, with all the same members, so assignments still work. This just then takes the internal struct and re-assigns things into our output struct.\n\nSpecifically, in the example below this is the part that does `return v.tint_symbol;` as we only have a single return value.\n\n```\nstruct tint_symbol_4 {\n  tint_symbol : vec4\u003cf32\u003e,\n  tint_symbol_1 : f32,\n  tint_symbol_2 : array\u003cf32, 1u\u003e,\n  tint_symbol_3 : array\u003cf32, 1u\u003e,\n}\n\nvar\u003cprivate\u003e v : tint_symbol_4;\n\nfn main_inner() {\n  v.tint_symbol \u003d vec4\u003cf32\u003e();\n}\n\n@vertex\nfn main() -\u003e @builtin(position) vec4\u003cf32\u003e {\n  main_inner();\n  return v.tint_symbol;\n}\n```\n\nAs an example (and this won\u0027t be emitted as we won\u0027t emit __cull_distance) but if there were two things in the emission struct you\u0027d see something like the below `  return tint_symbol_5(v.tint_symbol, v.tint_symbol_3);`:\n\n```\nstruct tint_symbol_4 {\n  tint_symbol : vec4\u003cf32\u003e,\n  tint_symbol_1 : f32,\n  tint_symbol_2 : array\u003cf32, 1u\u003e,\n  tint_symbol_3 : array\u003cf32, 1u\u003e,\n}\n\nvar\u003cprivate\u003e v : tint_symbol_4;\n\nfn main_inner() {\n  v.tint_symbol \u003d vec4\u003cf32\u003e();\n}\n\nstruct tint_symbol_5 {\n  @builtin(position)\n  tint_symbol : vec4\u003cf32\u003e,\n  @builtin(__cull_distance)\n  tint_symbol_3 : array\u003cf32, 1u\u003e,\n}\n\n@vertex\nfn main() -\u003e tint_symbol_5 {\n  main_inner();\n  return tint_symbol_5(v.tint_symbol, v.tint_symbol_3);\n}\n```\n\nSo, this check is what is causing the `tint_symbol_3` to not get emitted at the end of `main` in the first example. The inner struct still exists, and has the same members, so all access chains will have the same indices.",
      "parentUuid": "d12f9bb8_4a2b1c55",
      "revId": "f1c28fe8671ffba7ea7b5411b6ed0615a140d9b2",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
